   250→\subsubsection{Spatial Uncertainty Patterns}
   251→
   252→Spatial analysis of uncertainty maps reveals physically meaningful patterns with elevated uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries, areas with complex topographic features, and regions affected by atmospheric interference. The uncertainty framework provides interpretable confidence estimates for downstream applications, with uncertainty varying appropriately based on data quality and surface characteristics. Spatial autocorrelation analysis using Moran's I statistic yields a value of $0.548$ ($p = 0.002$), indicating moderate positive spatial autocorrelation. This confirms physically realistic spatial patterns where neighboring pixels exhibit correlated predictions, consistent with the continuous nature of snow cover distribution. The statistically significant autocorrelation validates that the model captures genuine spatial structure rather than producing spatially random predictions.
   253→
   254→\subsubsection{Uncertainty-Error Correlation}
   255→
   256→Analysis of the relationship between predicted uncertainty and prediction errors reveals strong negative correlation ($r = -0.563, p < 0.001$), confirming that higher uncertainty appropriately corresponds to larger prediction errors. This inverse relationship validates the utility of uncertainty estimates for identifying unreliable predictions in operational scenarios, enabling quality-controlled snow cover mapping where low-confidence predictions can be flagged for additional validation.
   257→
   258→\subsection{Qualitative Results}
   259→
   260→\begin{figure}[!h]
   261→    \centering
   262→    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images//results/Prediction_main_result_s02.png}
   263→    \caption{Representative qualitative results showing Sentinel-1 SAR dual-polarization backscatter (VV and VH) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery as model inputs (top row). Model outputs (bottom row) show fractional snow cover predictions, binary classification results, and spatially coherent uncertainty maps. The model successfully captures fine-scale spatial patterns with sharp boundaries at snow-land transitions. Uncertainty estimates appropriately increase at transition zones while maintaining low values in homogeneous regions, demonstrating excellent model calibration with uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries and complex terrain.}
   264→    \label{fig:qualitative}
   265→\end{figure}
   266→
   267→Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative} presents representative test samples demonstrating our model's superior spatial detail preservation and uncertainty quantification. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative_comparison}, our hybrid architecture produces smoother, more realistic snow cover maps with accurate boundary delineation. Early Fusion exhibits noisy predictions with false positives in non-snow regions due to its inability to capture complex inter-modal relationships. CNN-Only achieves better smoothness but misses fine-scale snow patterns in complex terrain due to lack of global context. Transformer-Only produces spatially inconsistent results, lacking the local feature extraction necessary for precise boundary localization. XFuse preserves fine-scale snow boundaries, avoids false positives in forested areas, and captures gradual snow-cover gradients more faithfully than all baselines. The corresponding per-model error maps (Figure~\ref{fig:error_maps}) reveal that XFuse achieves substantially lower absolute errors at snow-land transition zones and forest edges --- precisely the regions where spatial context from the Transformer and bidirectional cross-attention provide the greatest benefit. The spatially coherent uncertainty maps provide operationally valuable confidence information, with elevated uncertainty at ambiguous boundaries and low uncertainty in homogeneous regions.
