   283→\subsection{Robustness to Cloud Contamination}
   284→\label{sec:cloud_robustness}
   285→
   286→\added{Cloud contamination is the primary motivation for SAR-optical fusion: optical sensors cannot penetrate cloud cover, while Sentinel-1 SAR provides all-weather imaging capability. We validate XFuse's cloud robustness through two complementary analyses.}
   287→
   288→\added{\textbf{Architectural robustness via quality-aware training: } Our quality-aware training framework (Section~\ref{sec:quality_aware}) explicitly incorporates Sentinel-2 Scene Classification Layer (SCL) cloud masks as per-pixel loss weights during training. Pixels flagged as cloud-contaminated (SCL classes 7--9: cloud low/medium/ high probability; class 10: thin cirrus; class 3: cloud shadow) receive down-weighted loss contributions, while SAR observations, unaffected by clouds, are weighted by backscatter reliability. This ensures the model learns to leverage SAR structure when optical signals are degraded, rather than relying on optical artefacts. Critically, this quality-aware weighting comes from independently derived metadata (SCL from Sentinel-2 processing chain; SAR quality from radiometric calibration), providing multi-source evidence of data reliability.}
   289→
   290→\added{\textbf{Controlled cloud occlusion experiment:} To directly measure SAR's compensating role, we conduct a controlled \textit{simulated cloud occlusion} experiment: all Sentinel-2 input channels are set to zero for every test patch (simulating total optical loss), leaving Sentinel-1 SAR as the sole information source. This controlled design isolates SAR's contribution precisely and avoids confounds introduced by partial cloud coverage.}
   291→
   292→\added{Table~\ref{tab:cloud_robustness} presents results on all 18,117 test patches. XFuse under complete S2 occlusion achieves \textbf{83.2\%} accuracy, only 8.7 percentage points below full S1+S2 performance and \textbf{higher than Early Fusion operating with its complete multi-modal input (81.3\%)}. This result demonstrates that the bidirectional cross-attention mechanism internalises complementary SAR-optical representations: when optical data is unavailable, attention weights shift to prioritise S1 features, maintaining meaningful snow discrimination. Concatenation-based baselines (Early Fusion, CNN-Only) have no analogous adaptive mechanism: they treat both modalities symmetrically and cannot compensate for a missing modality.}
