   315→\subsection{Test-Time Augmentation and Ensemble Robustness}
   316→
   317→\subsubsection{TTA Performance Enhancement}
   318→
   319→Test-time augmentation (TTA) implementation significantly enhances prediction reliability across all test samples. Eight-fold geometric augmentation (rotation, reflection) improves accuracy by 2.3\% on average while reducing uncertainty by 22.4\%. This ensemble approach provides robust prediction intervals with accuracy gains ranging from 1.5\% to 3.0\% across different samples, confirming the value of TTA for operational snow monitoring applications.
   320→
   321→\begin{figure}[!h]
   322→    \centering
   323→    \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{images//results/tta_analysis_s00.png}
   324→    \caption{Test-time augmentation analysis. Eight-fold geometric augmentation (rotation, reflection) improves accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%, demonstrating enhanced prediction reliability through ensemble averaging.}
   325→    \label{fig:tta_analysis}
   326→\end{figure}
   327→
   328→\subsubsection{Ensemble Variance Reduction}
   329→
   330→Ensemble variance analysis demonstrates successful uncertainty reduction through augmentation averaging. The 22.4\% reduction in uncertainty variance indicates that TTA effectively captures prediction variability arising from orientation-dependent features, while maintaining prediction accuracy. This enhancement validates the importance of rotational and reflectional invariance in snow cover mapping applications.
   331→
   332→\subsection{Computational Efficiency}
   333→
   334→\textbf{Model Complexity.} Our hybrid architecture contains 47.1M parameters, compared to 12.8M (Early Fusion), 26.2M (CNN-Only), and 21.3M (Transformer-Only). Despite increased complexity, the performance gains justify the additional parameters: 38.7\% MAE reduction over CNN-Only represents substantial practical improvement for operational snow monitoring. Training completed in 34.6 hours on a single NVIDIA A30 GPU with batch size 8, demonstrating feasibility for standard research infrastructure.
   335→
   336→\textbf{Inference Speed.} The model processes 16$\times$16 patches (960m$\times$960m ground coverage at 60m resolution) with $\sim$50ms inference time per patch on A100 GPU. For the entire test set (18,117 patches), inference requires $\sim$15 minutes. Batch processing with batch size 32 achieves throughput of $\sim$2,000 patches/minute, enabling rapid large-scale deployment. Peak GPU memory consumption is 4.2GB during inference, allowing deployment on consumer-grade hardware.
   337→
   338→\textbf{Scalability.} For full satellite tile processing, sliding window extraction with configurable stride enables trade-off between spatial coverage and computational cost. The model's multi-resolution design naturally handles varying input sizes, supporting operational deployment at continental scales. Test-time augmentation increases inference time by 8$\times$ but provides significant accuracy and uncertainty improvements, making it suitable for high-stakes operational applications where prediction reliability is critical.
   339→
   340→\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}
   341→
   342→\textbf{Dataset Scope.} Our test set covers \deleted{4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025}\added{4 geographically distinct W-band MGRS tiles from northern Finland (December 2024--July 2025)}, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. Future work should evaluate on multi-season, multi-region datasets to assess year-round operational readiness and transferability to different snow conditions (maritime vs. continental snow, high-altitude vs. lowland environments).
   343→
   344→\textbf{Comparison Scope.} We compare against three baseline architectures trained on identical data and reference published methods where metrics are available. Comprehensive comparison with operational snow products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F, Sentinel-3 SLSTR) and additional fusion architectures remains future work. Our focus on architectural innovations and systematic ablation studies aligns with demonstrating methodological contributions, but operational validation requires direct comparison with existing operational systems.
   345→
   346→\textbf{Application Domain.} While we demonstrate effectiveness for fractional snow cover mapping, our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture with cross-modal attention, multi-resolution processing, and quality-aware learning should transfer to other remote sensing tasks (land cover classification, change detection, crop monitoring, disaster assessment). Evaluating task transferability and domain adaptation represents promising future research directions.
