   112→\textit{``In terms of writing, including the abstract and the methodology sections, the manuscript does not sufficiently distill and highlight the core contributions. Overall, it reads more like a technical report than a scientific paper, lacking clear summarization of the main ideas and insights.''}
   113→\end{quote}
   114→
   115→We thank the reviewer for this constructive feedback. We agree that the original manuscript was overly dense with technical details and numerical results, making it difficult to identify the core scientific contributions. We have substantially revised both the abstract and methodology sections to address this concern.
   116→
   117→\textbf{Abstract Revision:} The original abstract (~350 words) was restructured and condensed to ~250 words. Key changes include:
   118→\begin{itemize}
   119→    \item \textbf{Contribution-focused structure:} Instead of mixing problem description with exhaustive results, we now present three clearly enumerated contributions: (1) bidirectional cross-attention fusion, (2) multi-resolution training strategy ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$), and (3) quality-aware curriculum learning.
