   241→\section*{Response to Reviewer 2}
   242→
   243→\textbf{R2-1: Evaluation protocol for 10m predictions against 60m ground truth}
   244→\begin{quote}
   245→\textit{``Since the ground truth (GFSC) is 60 m, how are the 10 m predictions evaluated quantitatively? Did the authors downsample their 10m predictions back to 60 m to calculate MAE against the GFSC labels? Or did they upsample the GFSC labels to 10m? [...] The authors should ideally provide a small subset of `true' high-resolution ground truth to demonstrate that the model actually recovers 10 m details and not just artifacts.''}
   246→\end{quote}
   247→
   248→We thank the reviewer for raising this important methodological question, which deserves explicit clarification. We have added a new paragraph titled ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' in Section 4.1 to address this concern directly.
   249→
   250→\textbf{Answer to the specific questions:}
   251→
   252→\begin{enumerate}
   253→    \item \textbf{How are predictions evaluated?} The model is trained and evaluated entirely at \textbf{60m resolution}. We do \textit{not} downsample 10m predictions to 60m, nor do we upsample GFSC labels to 10m.
   254→
   255→    \item \textbf{The actual workflow:}
   256→    \begin{itemize}
   257→        \item Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are \textbf{aggregated to 60m} through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks before being fed to the model
   258→        \item The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution
   259→        \item All metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, $R^2$, etc.) are computed at 60m against GFSC labels
   260→        \item This is implemented in our codebase: \texttt{aggregate\_10m\_to\_60m()} function applies mean aggregation over $6 \times 6$ blocks (e.g., 100$\times$100 pixels at 10m $\rightarrow$ 16$\times$16 pixels at 60m)
   261→    \end{itemize}
   262→
   263→    \item \textbf{What about the ``10m prediction'' claim?} The multi-resolution framework ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) enables optional \textit{inference-time} disaggregation from 60m predictions back to 10m through guided upsampling that leverages high-resolution input features. This is a capability for operational deployment, not part of the training/validation pipeline.
   264→\end{enumerate}
   265→
   266→\textbf{Clarification added to manuscript:}
   267→
   268→We have added the following explicit statement in Section 4.1 (Experimental Setup):
   269→
   270→\begin{quote}
   271→``During training, Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are aggregated to 60m through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks to match the GFSC reference data resolution. The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution, and \textbf{all quantitative metrics reported in this paper are computed at 60m resolution} against GFSC labels. [...] However, we emphasize that the 10m outputs represent \textit{spatially enhanced predictions} guided by fine-scale input structure, not independently validated high-resolution products.''
   272→\end{quote}
   273→
   274→\textbf{Regarding high-resolution ground truth:}
   275→
   276→The reviewer correctly notes that ideally we would validate 10m predictions against true high-resolution ground truth. We acknowledge this limitation explicitly:
   277→
   278→\begin{itemize}
   279→    \item High-resolution ($<$60m) fractional snow cover ground truth is not widely available for our study region
   280→    \item The 10m predictions are validated \textit{qualitatively} by visual alignment with Sentinel-2 optical imagery boundaries (Figure 8 in the manuscript shows boundary preservation)
   281→    \item \textit{Quantitative} validation of sub-60m accuracy would require independent high-resolution ground truth from UAV surveys, airborne sensors, or Landsat-derived 30m products
   282→    \item We have added this as an explicit limitation and future work direction in Section 5.5
   283→\end{itemize}
   284→
   285→We believe this transparent clarification addresses the reviewer's concern. The methodology is scientifically sound: training at the resolution of available ground truth is standard practice in remote sensing, and the guided disaggregation capability provides operational value even without high-resolution validation.
   286→
   287→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 4.1 (new ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' paragraph); Section 5.5 Limitation \#4 (expanded to clarify evaluation resolution).
   288→
   289→\textbf{R2-2: Geographic generalization concerns (alpine terrain)}
   290→\begin{quote}
   291→\textit{``The study focuses on 29 tiles in Northern Finland. [...] How does XFuse handle complex alpine terrain (Alps or Himalayas) where SAR geometric distortions are severe? The current dataset might not sufficiently challenge the cross-attention fusion mechanism's ability to handle missing SAR information due to shadows.''}
   292→\end{quote}
   293→We thank the reviewer for raising this critical question, which addresses perhaps the most significant limitation of our current evaluation. The reviewer is entirely correct: northern Finland's boreal landscape represents a favorable scenario for SAR-based fusion, and we agree that geographic transferability is the primary open question for our methodology. We have added a substantial new ``Geographic Transferability'' paragraph as Limitation \#2 in Section~5.5 that addresses each of the reviewer's specific concerns in detail.
   294→
   295→\textbf{(1) SAR Geometric Distortions in Alpine Terrain:}
   296→
   297→Northern Finland's gentle topography (typical slopes $<$5\textdegree) effectively minimizes the geometric distortions that plague SAR observations in mountainous regions. In alpine terrain, the interaction between Sentinel-1's nominal incidence angles (29\textdegree--46\textdegree) and steep slopes produces three distinct degradation mechanisms:
   298→\begin{itemize}
   299→    \item \textit{Foreshortening:} On foreslopes exceeding $\sim$20\textdegree, topographic relief is compressed in the range direction, degrading spatial resolution and mixing returns from different elevations.
   300→    \item \textit{Layover:} When slope angle approaches the complement of the incidence angle, returns from multiple ground positions collapse into the same range cell, producing physically uninterpretable pixels.
   301→    \item \textit{Radar shadow:} Steep back-slopes receive no radar illumination, creating complete signal voids.
   302→\end{itemize}
   303→These effects concentrate in precisely the high-altitude accumulation zones where snow monitoring is most critical for water resource management. Our results should therefore be interpreted as achievable under near-optimal SAR acquisition geometry.
   304→
   305→\textbf{(2) Snow Metamorphism and Regime-Dependent SAR Signatures:}
   306→
   307→The reviewer correctly identifies that SAR-snow interactions are highly dependent on snow type. Our study area's predominantly dry continental snow exhibits relatively stable C-band backscatter. In contrast:
   308→\begin{itemize}
   309→    \item \textit{Maritime snowpacks} (warm, dense, frequent melt--freeze) produce strong C-band absorption through liquid water content, causing dramatic backscatter changes during diurnal cycles.
   310→    \item \textit{Continental interior snowpacks} (cold, dry, faceted grains) exhibit volume scattering from depth hoar layers.
   311→    \item \textit{Transitional regimes} (e.g., Himalayan basins) display complex elevation-dependent diurnal stratification.
   312→\end{itemize}
   313→Feature representations learned from boreal Finland may not generalize to these different physical regimes without recalibration or domain adaptation.
   314→
   315→\textbf{(3) How the Architecture Could Address These Challenges:}
   316→
   317→While we have not validated XFuse in alpine terrain, two architectural design choices are directly relevant to the reviewer's concern about ``handling missing SAR information'':
   318→\begin{itemize}
   319→    \item The \textit{bidirectional cross-attention mechanism} enables each modality to selectively attend to the complementary modality through learned query-key-value projections. In SAR-degraded regions (shadow, layover), the cross-attention weights could theoretically shift toward optical spectral features-analogous to the mechanism's designed behavior under cloud contamination, but operating in the reverse modality direction. The asymmetric nature of cross-attention (each modality independently queries the other) is specifically designed for scenarios where one sensor provides degraded information.
   320→    \item The \textit{quality-aware training framework} already learns continuous sample weighting from heterogeneous metadata sources (SCL, AOT, WVP, QC flags). This framework provides a natural extension point: terrain-derived quality indicators-local incidence angle, DEM-based layover/shadow masks-could be incorporated as additional metadata channels, enabling the model to systematically down-weight geometrically distorted SAR observations during training.
   321→\end{itemize}
   322→However, we are careful to present these as \textit{architectural affordances} rather than demonstrated capabilities. Without validation on mountainous datasets, we cannot claim that the cross-attention mechanism will successfully adapt to SAR shadow regions in practice.
   323→
   324→\textbf{(4) Concrete Path Forward:}
   325→
   326→We identify four requirements for robust geographic generalization in the revised manuscript:
   327→\begin{enumerate}
   328→    \item Radiometric terrain correction of Sentinel-1 data using high-resolution DEMs
   329→    \item Incorporation of topographic variables (slope, aspect, curvature, sky view factor) as auxiliary inputs
   330→    \item Training on geographically diverse datasets spanning boreal, alpine, and maritime snow regimes
   331→    \item Domain adaptation techniques for efficient transfer to undersampled snow climate zones
   332→\end{enumerate}
   333→We frame multi-region validation as the primary direction for future work and state explicitly that our current results represent performance under favorable SAR conditions.
   334→
   335→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section~5.5 (new Limitation \#2: ``Geographic Transferability''-comprehensive discussion of SAR geometric distortions in alpine terrain, snow metamorphism regime effects, architectural mitigation properties, and concrete future work requirements).
   336→
   337→\textbf{R2-3: Baseline comparison fairness (multi-resolution head)}
   338→\begin{quote}
   339→\textit{``Are these baselines also equipped with the Multi-Resolution Processing (10m$\to$60m$\to$10m) head? If XFuse has the advantage of the guided upsampling head while baselines simply interpolate 60m predictions, the comparison is unfair.''}
   340→\end{quote}
   341→We appreciate this important question regarding experimental fairness. We confirm that \textbf{all baselines are trained and evaluated under identical conditions}, ensuring a fair comparison:
   342→
   343→\begin{itemize}
   344→    \item \textbf{Identical data pipeline:} All models-Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only, and XFuse-use the same dataset class (\texttt{EnhancedDualResolutionDataset} with \\ \texttt{train\_mode=True}), which aggregates Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs from native 10m resolution to 60m via mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks. No model receives higher-resolution inputs than any other.
   345→    \item \textbf{Identical training targets:} All models predict against the same 60m GFSC reference labels. All quantitative metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, F1, $R^2$) are computed at 60m resolution.
   346→    \item \textbf{Multi-resolution head is inference-only:} The guided disaggregation ($60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) described in Section~3.2 is a post-processing step applied \textit{after} the core prediction during inference. It is \textbf{not} used during training or quantitative evaluation, and therefore does not contribute to any reported metric.
   347→    \item \textbf{Ablation evidence confirms architectural gains:} The ablation study (Table~3) shows that removing multi-resolution processing reduces accuracy by only 1.76 percentage points (94.66\% $\rightarrow$ 92.90\%). Critically, XFuse \textit{without} multi-resolution processing (92.90\%) still outperforms the best baseline (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) by 3.77 percentage points, confirming that the performance gains are attributable to the cross-attention fusion and transformer components, not the resolution strategy.
   348→\end{itemize}
   349→
   350→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Added explicit fairness clarification paragraph in Section~4.2; added isolation sentence in the ablation discussion (Section~4.4).
